 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-1.  **Loss of Trilinear Interpolation:** The primary limitation is that real trilinear interpolation of the volume data is not possible because the gradient and color/opacity data must reside in separate textures, and the hardware only supports two input textures at a time [S1, unofficial][S2]. This results in limited image quality compared to methods using 3D textures or multi-pass techniques [S1, unofficial][S2].
-2.  **No Per-Voxel Ambient Color with Trilinear Interpolation:** If one attempts to keep trilinear interpolation, the RGB channels are used for the gradient vector and the Alpha channel for opacity. This leaves no channel for per-voxel ambient color, forcing the use of a globally defined ambient color [S1, unofficial][S2].
-3.  **Hardware Limits on Signed Vectors:** Early hardware required workarounds for signed vector components (gradients). This method leverages hardware support for signed vectors, avoiding large-scale workarounds [S2].
-4.  **Limited Lighting:** The described single-pass setup supports one diffuse light source efficiently. A second colored light source can be computed using unused registers (C and D of the first combiner stage), but this requires sacrificing either trilinear interpolation or separate ambient/diffuse color specification [S1, unofficial].
